{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"9fc9be51-89a5-4ddb-9aab-91d350910a8f","title":"A system of two linear equations as crossing lines"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9fc9be51-89a5-4ddb-9aab-91d350910a8f","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Each equation draws a line. A solution to the system must lie on both lines at once.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Two equations, one shared point"},{"op":"equation","id":"first","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=x+1"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-1,5],"y":[-1,6],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"rising","y":"x+1","domain":[-1,5],"label":"y = x + 1"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9fc9be51-89a5-4ddb-9aab-91d350910a8f","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Now add the second equation. Where the lines cross, the same x and y satisfy both equations.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"second","anchor":{"el":"first","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=-x+5"},{"op":"figure","id":"addline","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"falling","y":"-x+5","domain":[-1,5],"label":"y = −x + 5"},{"kind":"point","id":"crossing","at":{"hit":["rising","falling"]},"label":"(2, 3)"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why is the crossing point a solution to both equations?","expected_gist":"It lies on both lines, so its coordinates satisfy both equations."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9fc9be51-89a5-4ddb-9aab-91d350910a8f","step":{"id":"s3","script":"At the crossing, both expressions for y are equal. Solving gives x equals two; substituting gives y equals three.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"solve","anchor":{"el":"second","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x+1=-x+5\\;\\Rightarrow\\;2x=4\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x=2"},{"op":"equation","id":"substitute","anchor":{"el":"solve","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=2+1=3"},{"op":"figure","id":"guides","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"crossing"},"to":[2,0],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"crossing"},"to":[0,3],"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9fc9be51-89a5-4ddb-9aab-91d350910a8f","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Check the point in both equations. Both are true, so the system has exactly one solution: the crossing point, two comma three.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"check","anchor":{"el":"substitute","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"3=2+1,\\qquad 3=-2+5"},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"check","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{(x,y)=(2,3)}"},{"op":"figure","id":"highlight","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"circle","center":{"at":"crossing"},"r":0.22},{"kind":"text","at":[3.1,2.4],"text":"one solution","size":"small"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
